###  https://w3id.org/tvbo/system_size
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/system_size> rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ;
                                    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl> ;
                                    rdfs:label "system_size (slot)" ;
                                    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "System size (van Kampen Ω) for the stochastic 'gillespie' backend: the number of discrete units per unit of the activity variable, and the sole source of noise. Runs a mean-field rate model as a finite birth-death process (Gillespie SSA): the activity state variable X becomes a discrete count n ≈ Ω·X, its relaxation equation tau*X' = -X + F(...) is read as birth propensity Ω·F/tau (the gain) and death propensity n/tau (the leak), while the remaining state variables integrate deterministically between events. Larger Ω means finer discreteness and weaker fluctuations, recovering the deterministic mean field as Ω→∞. For an activity already expressed as a fraction in [0,1], Ω is the population size N; for a firing rate it is N divided by the rate at full activation. Required by the 'gillespie' backend; ignored by every other backend." ;
                                    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://w3id.org/tvbo> ;
                                    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "system_size" .


###  https://w3id.org/tvbo/tail_duration
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/tail_duration> rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ;
                                      rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl> ;
                                      rdfs:label "tail_duration (slot)" ;
                                      <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The trailing span of simulated time to aggregate over, in the same time unit as the integration step. Converted to a sample count against the spacing of the samples it is cut from, so the window stays the same length of simulated time at any step: this observation's own sample period (its `period`/`downsample_period`, else the integration `step_size`) for a recorded trajectory, and the integration `step_size` for a `reduce: streaming` reducer or a tuning loop, which fold every step. On an algorithm's observation the span may cover several iterations: a duration longer than the algorithm's `simulation_period` must be a whole multiple of it, and the observation is then the mean over that many most recent periods, or over every period so far while fewer have run. Mutually exclusive with `tail_samples`." ;
                                      <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://w3id.org/tvbo> ;
                                      <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "tail_duration" .


###  https://w3id.org/tvbo/tail_samples
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/tail_samples> rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty .


###  https://w3id.org/tvbo/tail_x
<https://w3id.org/tvbo/tail_x> rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty ;
                               rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/tvbo/tvbo.owl> ;
                               rdfs:label "tail_x (slot)" ;
                               <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Start the arrow at this x in DATA coordinates instead of at an axes-fraction offset. Pair with tail_used, which supplies the y: together they anchor the arrow's tail on a point the run computed, so a callout keeps pointing at its data when the data changes. Ignored unless tail_used is also set." ;
                               <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://w3id.org/tvbo/figure> ;
                               <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "tail_x" .


